IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `rtw-dates`

-- Return to Work Act 1986 (NT) -- the seat-neutral core: who is covered, what is paid, and the duties of the worker
-- and the employer on a claim.
--
-- Written in the Act's own actors and terms: "the worker" (s 3B; on a death claim, the dependant or other person making
-- the claim, the "claimant"), "the employer" (s 3(1); read with its insurer, and the Nominal Insurer where the employer is
-- uninsured, s 85(10)). Computations are functions. Where the text says "shall" or "must" or sets a period, there is a
-- regulative rule (DEONTIC) with the worker and the employer as parties. The worker's questions are in rtw-seat.l4 and the
-- employer's in rtw-seat-employer.l4; both IMPORT this module and restate no rule.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 3(1) ("average weekly earnings", "Territory worker"); s 3A; s 3B(1)-(6), (10), (11), (14), (15),
-- (17) with regs 3A, 4, 4A, 5; s 4 (as a fact, with s 4(2A) as a fact); s 49A; s 53(1), (2); s 53AA(1), (3); s 57; s 58
-- (as a fact); s 60; s 61A(2)(a); s 61B; s 62 with regs 6, 6AA; s 63; s 64; s 65(1)-(3A), (6)-(14); s 65A; s 65B with
-- reg 6A; s 65C; s 69(1); s 70; s 71; s 72(3); s 74; s 75B; s 80; s 82(1), (2), (6) with reg 12; s 84(1), (3), (5); s 85(1)-(4),
-- (6)(c), (8)-(10) with reg 13; s 85A; s 86; s 87; s 88; s 89 (the weeks only) with reg 14; s 103D(1A), (4), (5);
-- s 103J; s 104(3), (4); s 182.
--
-- Edition: reprint REPR050, as in force at 25 March 2024, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/Return-to-Work-Act-1986-NT-current-reprint-REPR050.txt; with the Return to Work
-- Regulations 1986, reprint REPR050R2, as in force at 1 July 2024, as deposited at
-- ../../../return-to-work-regulations-1986/registers/source-bundle/Return-to-Work-Regulations-1986-NT-current-reprint-REPR050R2.txt.
--
-- INPUTS, because the deposit does not hold them: average weekly earnings (s 3(1), published by the Australian
-- Statistician); the indexed normal weekly earnings (s 65(3), (3A): the formula did not survive conversion); the s 89
-- interest (the formula did not survive conversion; the reg 14 rate is the Supreme Court judgment-debt rate); the future
-- pension age (s 65(14), Social Security Act 1991 (Cth) s 23); the Territory's public holidays.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: Parts 2-3 and 7 (the Authority, the Council, inspectors, insurance, premiums, the Nominal Insurer's Fund);
-- s 50A firefighters and the prescribed-disease lists (Sch 2, regs 5AB, 5B, 5C) beyond the fact that the injury arose in
-- the course of employment; s 53A bodily fluids; s 54 other laws; s 55 diseases; s 56; s 61; s 65(4) apprentices and
-- juniors (inside the indexed earnings input); s 66 and reg 8 volunteers; s 68 (inside the earning capacity input);
-- s 69 (only its 14 days and statement, s 69(1)); s 72A; s 73 medical costs (beyond s 61A); Division 4 except s 75B; ss 78A-78B settlements;
-- ss 90-92; the Court's procedure; transitional Parts.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, with the design defaults
-- ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as
-- advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Return to Work Act 1986 (NT) -- seat-neutral core`

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser amount of` a `and` b MEANS IF a AT MOST b THEN a ELSE b

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the greater amount of` a `and` b MEANS IF a AT LEAST b THEN a ELSE b

§§ `s 3(1) -- average weekly earnings, and the annual equivalent`

-- s 3(1): "average weekly earnings means the Average Weekly Earnings for Full Time Adult Persons, Weekly Ordinary Time
-- Earnings for the Northern Territory last published by the Australian Statistician before 1 January before the date in
-- respect of which they are required under this Act to be assessed". Not deposited: every function takes it as an INPUT.
-- Regs 4A, 5 and 6 use "the annual equivalent of average weekly earnings", which neither the Act nor the Regulations
-- define. READING: 52 times the weekly figure.
`the weeks in a year, for the annual equivalent` MEANS 52

GIVEN `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the annual equivalent of` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    `average weekly earnings` TIMES `the weeks in a year, for the annual equivalent`

§§ `s 3B and regs 3A, 4, 4A, 5 -- who is a worker`

-- s 3B(17): "Despite anything in this section, a person, or a member of a class of persons, prescribed by regulation:
-- (a) as a worker is a worker for this Act; and (b) as not being a worker is not a worker for this Act."
-- Reg 3A(1) prescribes as workers: members of the operations branch of St John Ambulance Australia (NT); jurors; individuals
-- authorised by a club registered under the Racing and Wagering Act 2024; drivers of taxis, private hire cars, limousines
-- or buses engaged by an accredited and approved operator. Reg 3A(2) prescribes as not workers: fishing crew paid wholly or
-- mainly by a share of the catch; approved direct selling agents; carers under the Care and Protection of Children Act
-- 2007; people caring for reward for another person's child in their own home.
DECLARE `Prescribed class` IS ONE OF
    `prescribed as a worker by reg 3A(1)`
    `prescribed as not a worker by reg 3A(2)`
    `in neither prescribed class`

DECLARE `Worker facts` HAS
    `works for another person under a contract`                                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `is an employee under the PAYG provisions, or should be`                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `is employed by the Commonwealth`                                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `employs another person in the work`                                                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `is engaged other than for the purposes of the employer's trade, business or enterprise`  IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employer makes PAYG withholding payments for the person`                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a prescribed family member of the employer and lives with the employer`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a director of the body corporate that employs the person`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `PAYG is withheld from the director's remuneration`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `the person's details were disclosed to the employer's insurer as s 3B(4) or (6)(b) requires` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is employed or engaged by a householder`                                                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `is under the contract a contestant in a sporting or athletic activity, or training or travelling for one` IS A BOOLEAN
    `yearly remuneration`                                                                     IS A NUMBER
    `prescribed class`                                                                        IS A `Prescribed class`

-- Reg 4A: for s 3B(11), "the prescribed amount is 20% of the annual equivalent of average weekly earnings".
-- Reg 5: for s 3B(15), "the prescribed amount is 65% of the annual equivalent of average weekly earnings".
`the reg 4A share of the annual equivalent, for a householder's worker` MEANS 20%
`the reg 5 share of the annual equivalent, for a sporting contestant`   MEANS 65%

-- s 3B(1): "A worker is an individual who: (a) performs work or a service of any kind for another person (the employer)
-- under a contract; and (b) ... (i) is an employee for the purpose of assessment for PAYG withholding ...; or (ii) despite
-- that the employer does not treat a person as an employee, the person should be an employee under the PAYG provisions."
-- (2): not "(a) ... employed by the Commonwealth; or (b) ... an employer of another person engaged in the performance of
-- the work or service; or (c) ... employed or engaged other than for the purposes of the employer's trade, business or
-- enterprise and in respect of whom the employer does not make any withholding payments under the PAYG provisions."
-- (3), (4): a prescribed family member (reg 4: spouse, parent, step-parent, grandparent, child, stepchild, grandchild,
-- brother or sister, half or whole) who lives with the employer is not a worker unless disclosed to the insurer.
-- (5), (6): a director is not a worker unless PAYG is withheld from the remuneration and the director is disclosed.
-- (10), (11): a householder's worker is a worker only if the individual "earns more than, or is paid at a rate that would
-- exceed, the prescribed amount". (14), (15): a contestant is not a worker unless entitled to "remuneration of not less
-- than the prescribed amount per year". READING: (10)-(15) take out people who meet (1) and (2); they do not make a worker
-- of someone who does not.
GIVEN `the person` IS A `Worker facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is a worker under s 3B` IF
    CONSIDER `the person`'s `prescribed class`
    WHEN `prescribed as a worker by reg 3A(1)`     THEN TRUE
    WHEN `prescribed as not a worker by reg 3A(2)` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE
            `s 3B(1) is met`
        AND (NOT `s 3B(2) takes the person out`)
        AND (NOT `s 3B(3) takes the person out`)
        AND (NOT `s 3B(5) takes the person out`)
        AND (NOT `s 3B(10) takes the person out`)
        AND (NOT `s 3B(14) takes the person out`)
    WHERE
        `s 3B(1) is met` MEANS
                `the person`'s `works for another person under a contract`
            AND `the person`'s `is an employee under the PAYG provisions, or should be`
        `s 3B(2) takes the person out` MEANS
                `the person`'s `is employed by the Commonwealth`
            OR  `the person`'s `employs another person in the work`
            OR  (    `the person`'s `is engaged other than for the purposes of the employer's trade, business or enterprise`
                 AND (NOT (`the person`'s `the employer makes PAYG withholding payments for the person`)))
        `s 3B(3) takes the person out` MEANS
                `the person`'s `is a prescribed family member of the employer and lives with the employer`
            AND (NOT (`the person`'s `the person's details were disclosed to the employer's insurer as s 3B(4) or (6)(b) requires`))
        `s 3B(5) takes the person out` MEANS
                `the person`'s `is a director of the body corporate that employs the person`
            AND (NOT (    `the person`'s `PAYG is withheld from the director's remuneration`
                      AND `the person`'s `the person's details were disclosed to the employer's insurer as s 3B(4) or (6)(b) requires`))
        `s 3B(10) takes the person out` MEANS
                `the person`'s `is employed or engaged by a householder`
            AND (NOT (`the person`'s `yearly remuneration` GREATER THAN (`the reg 4A share of the annual equivalent, for a householder's worker` TIMES `the annual equivalent of` `average weekly earnings`)))
        `s 3B(14) takes the person out` MEANS
                `the person`'s `is under the contract a contestant in a sporting or athletic activity, or training or travelling for one`
            AND (NOT (`the person`'s `yearly remuneration` AT LEAST (`the reg 5 share of the annual equivalent, for a sporting contestant` TIMES `the annual equivalent of` `average weekly earnings`)))

§§ `ss 53, 53AA -- employment connected with the Territory`

DECLARE `Jurisdiction` IS ONE OF `the Territory`, `another State or Territory`, `no one jurisdiction`

-- s 53AA(1): "A worker's employment is connected with a particular jurisdiction if: (a) the worker usually works in that
-- employment in that jurisdiction; or (b) if no jurisdiction or no one jurisdiction is identified by paragraph (a) -- the
-- worker is usually based in the jurisdiction for the purposes of that employment; or (c) if no jurisdiction or no one
-- jurisdiction is identified by paragraph (a) or (b) -- the principal place of business in Australia of the worker's
-- employer is located in the jurisdiction." (3): if none is identified, the employment is connected with the Territory if
-- "(a) the worker is in the Territory when the worker suffers an injury; and (b) there is no place outside Australia under
-- the legislation of which the worker may be entitled to compensation for the same matter." (4)-(7) guide the findings
-- (12 months' history, intentions, temporary arrangements of 6 months or less ignored), which are asked as facts. The ship
-- rule in (2) is not encoded.
DECLARE `Connection facts` HAS
    `where the worker usually works in the employment`                      IS A `Jurisdiction`
    `where the worker is usually based for the employment`                  IS A `Jurisdiction`
    `where the employer's principal place of business in Australia is`      IS A `Jurisdiction`
    `the worker was in the Territory when injured`                          IS A BOOLEAN
    `a place outside Australia may compensate the worker for the same matter` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN j IS A `Jurisdiction`, `the next test` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Territory is identified by` j `or else by` `the next test` IF
    CONSIDER j
    WHEN `the Territory`               THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another State or Territory`  THEN FALSE
    WHEN `no one jurisdiction`         THEN `the next test`

GIVEN c IS A `Connection facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employment is connected with the Territory under s 53AA` IF
    `the Territory is identified by` (c's `where the worker usually works in the employment`) `or else by`
        (`the Territory is identified by` (c's `where the worker is usually based for the employment`) `or else by`
            (`the Territory is identified by` (c's `where the employer's principal place of business in Australia is`) `or else by`
                (    c's `the worker was in the Territory when injured`
                 AND (NOT (c's `a place outside Australia may compensate the worker for the same matter`)))))

§§ `ss 3A, 4, 53, 57, 58, 60 -- is the injury compensable`

-- s 3A(1): an injury "is a physical or mental injury arising out of or in the course of the worker's employment", including
-- a disease and the aggravation of a pre-existing injury or disease. (2): a mental injury is not an injury "if it is caused
-- wholly or primarily by" reasonable management action, a reasonable decision about it, or the worker's expectation of it.
-- s 4 deems many injuries to arise in the course of employment (at work, recesses, training, medical attendance, journeys
-- by the shortest convenient route, gradual process, prescribed diseases) and is asked as a fact; s 4(2A) takes out an
-- injury "sustained in a motor accident" in a recess or on most journeys, which the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979
-- then covers. s 53(1): compensation is payable "if a Territory worker suffers an injury ... and that injury results in or
-- materially contributes to his or her: (a) death; (b) impairment; or (c) incapacity". s 53(2): "only in respect of
-- employment that is connected with the Territory". s 58: a heart attack or stroke injury is compensable only if the
-- employment materially contributed (asked as a fact; s 58(2) lists what must be weighed). s 57: not payable for an injury
-- "(a) that was deliberately self inflicted; or (b) (not being an injury resulting in his or her death or permanent or
-- long-term incapacity) attributable to his or her serious and wilful misconduct".
-- s 60(1): "A worker is not entitled to compensation in respect of an injury sustained whilst driving a motor vehicle ...
-- after having consumed alcoholic liquor where the consumption of that liquor materially contributed to the accident ...,
-- or while under the influence of a drug." (2): contribution is presumed, unless the contrary is proved, at 0.08 grams or
-- more per 100 millilitres of blood or per 210 litres of breath. (3): "Nothing in this section affects an entitlement to
-- compensation under Subdivision A or D of Division 3 or Division 4" (death benefits; medical and other costs;
-- rehabilitation).
DECLARE `Injury facts` HAS
    `arose out of or in the course of the employment`                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a mental injury caused wholly or primarily by reasonable management action`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `was sustained in a motor accident that s 4(2A) takes out of the employment`               IS A BOOLEAN
    `resulted in or materially contributed to death, impairment or incapacity`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a heart attack or stroke injury`                                                      IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employment materially contributed to the heart attack or stroke`                     IS A BOOLEAN
    `was deliberately self-inflicted`                                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `is attributable to the worker's serious and wilful misconduct`                           IS A BOOLEAN
    `resulted in death or permanent or long-term incapacity`                                  IS A BOOLEAN
    `was sustained while the worker was driving a motor vehicle`                              IS A BOOLEAN
    `the worker had consumed alcoholic liquor`                                                IS A BOOLEAN
    `the worker's alcohol concentration, in grams per 100 millilitres of blood or per 210 litres of breath` IS A NUMBER
    `the consumption is found to have materially contributed to the accident`                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `the contrary of the s 60(2) presumption is proved`                                       IS A BOOLEAN
    `the worker was under the influence of a drug`                                            IS A BOOLEAN

`the s 60(2) alcohol concentration` MEANS 0.08

GIVEN i IS AN `Injury facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 60 applies to the injury` IF
        i's `was sustained while the worker was driving a motor vehicle`
    AND (   (    i's `the worker had consumed alcoholic liquor`
             AND (   i's `the consumption is found to have materially contributed to the accident`
                  OR (    i's `the worker's alcohol concentration, in grams per 100 millilitres of blood or per 210 litres of breath` AT LEAST `the s 60(2) alcohol concentration`
                      AND (NOT (i's `the contrary of the s 60(2) presumption is proved`)))))
         OR i's `the worker was under the influence of a drug`)

DECLARE `Coverage answer` IS ONE OF
    `compensable under Part 5`
    `compensable only for death benefits, medical and other costs and rehabilitation (s 60(3))`
    `not compensable: not a worker (s 3B)`
    `not compensable: the employment is not connected with the Territory (ss 53(2), 53AA)`
    `not compensable: not an injury arising out of or in the course of the employment (ss 3A(1), 4)`
    `not compensable: a mental injury caused wholly or primarily by reasonable management action (s 3A(2))`
    `not compensable: a motor accident in a recess or on a journey that s 4(2A) takes out`
    `not compensable: no death, impairment or incapacity (s 53(1))`
    `not compensable: a heart attack or stroke to which the employment did not materially contribute (s 58)`
    `not compensable: deliberately self-inflicted (s 57(a))`
    `not compensable: serious and wilful misconduct, and no death or permanent or long-term incapacity (s 57(b))`

GIVEN `the person` IS A `Worker facts`, c IS A `Connection facts`, i IS AN `Injury facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Coverage answer`
`whether the injury is compensable` `the person` c i `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the person is a worker under s 3B` `the person` `average weekly earnings`)
            THEN `not compensable: not a worker (s 3B)`
        IF NOT (`the employment is connected with the Territory under s 53AA` c)
            THEN `not compensable: the employment is not connected with the Territory (ss 53(2), 53AA)`
        IF NOT (i's `arose out of or in the course of the employment`)
            THEN `not compensable: not an injury arising out of or in the course of the employment (ss 3A(1), 4)`
        IF i's `is a mental injury caused wholly or primarily by reasonable management action`
            THEN `not compensable: a mental injury caused wholly or primarily by reasonable management action (s 3A(2))`
        IF i's `was sustained in a motor accident that s 4(2A) takes out of the employment`
            THEN `not compensable: a motor accident in a recess or on a journey that s 4(2A) takes out`
        IF NOT (i's `resulted in or materially contributed to death, impairment or incapacity`)
            THEN `not compensable: no death, impairment or incapacity (s 53(1))`
        IF i's `is a heart attack or stroke injury` AND (NOT (i's `the employment materially contributed to the heart attack or stroke`))
            THEN `not compensable: a heart attack or stroke to which the employment did not materially contribute (s 58)`
        IF i's `was deliberately self-inflicted`
            THEN `not compensable: deliberately self-inflicted (s 57(a))`
        IF i's `is attributable to the worker's serious and wilful misconduct` AND (NOT (i's `resulted in death or permanent or long-term incapacity`))
            THEN `not compensable: serious and wilful misconduct, and no death or permanent or long-term incapacity (s 57(b))`
        IF `s 60 applies to the injury` i
            THEN `compensable only for death benefits, medical and other costs and rehabilitation (s 60(3))`
        OTHERWISE `compensable under Part 5`

-- For the Motor Accidents (Compensation) Act 1979 s 10A(b): an injury that "would have been so compensable but for the
-- fact that the person's injury or death: (i) was deliberately self-inflicted; or (ii) is attributable to the person's
-- serious and wilful misconduct". Every earlier test is passed when the answer is one of the two s 57 answers.
GIVEN `the person` IS A `Worker facts`, c IS A `Connection facts`, i IS AN `Injury facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the injury would be compensable but for s 57` `the person` c i `average weekly earnings` IF
    CONSIDER `whether the injury is compensable` `the person` c i `average weekly earnings`
    WHEN `not compensable: deliberately self-inflicted (s 57(a))` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `not compensable: serious and wilful misconduct, and no death or permanent or long-term incapacity (s 57(b))` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN a IS A `Coverage answer`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the answer allows weekly payments and the impairment lump sum` a IF
    CONSIDER a
    WHEN `compensable under Part 5` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `ss 80, 182 -- notice of the injury, and the time for a claim`

-- s 182(1): proceedings for compensation "shall not be maintainable unless notice of the injury has been given before the
-- worker has voluntarily left the employment in which he or she was injured and unless the claim for compensation has been
-- made: (a) within 6 months after the occurrence of the injury or, in the case of a disease, the incapacity arising from the
-- disease; or (b) in the case of death, within 6 months after advice of the death has been received by the claimant."
-- (2): the want of notice is not a bar if the employer is not prejudiced, or it "was occasioned by mistake, absence from the
-- Territory or other reasonable cause". (3): "The failure to make a claim within the period ... shall not be a bar ... if it
-- is found that the failure was occasioned by mistake, ignorance of a disease, absence from the Territory or other
-- reasonable cause." (4): leaving only because the injury made the worker unable to continue is not leaving voluntarily.
-- (5): a payment the person believes to be compensation, and conduct of the employer or insurer leading the person to
-- believe compensation will be paid or is not available, "shall be taken to be a reasonable cause". The findings are the
-- Court's: inputs.
`the s 182(1) period, in months` MEANS 6

GIVEN `the day the period runs from` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to claim under s 182(1)` `the day the period runs from` MEANS
    `the day the period runs from` `plus months` `the s 182(1) period, in months`

DECLARE `Claim time facts` HAS
    `the day of the injury, or of the incapacity from a disease, or the day advice of the death was received` IS A DATE
    `the day the claim was made`                                                    IS A DATE
    `notice of the injury was given before the worker voluntarily left the employment` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employer is not prejudiced, or the want of notice had a reasonable cause`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the late claim is found to have been occasioned by mistake, ignorance of a disease, absence from the Territory or other reasonable cause` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the claimant was paid what they believed was compensation, or the employer or insurer led them to believe compensation would be paid or was not available` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Claim time answer` IS ONE OF
    `maintainable: notice given and the claim made within 6 months (s 182(1))`
    `maintainable: the want of notice or the late claim is excused (s 182(2), (3), (5))`
    `not maintainable: the claim was not made within 6 months and no reasonable cause is found (s 182(1), (3))`
    `not maintainable: no notice before the worker voluntarily left the employment, and s 182(2) does not excuse it`

GIVEN f IS A `Claim time facts`
GIVETH A `Claim time answer`
`whether proceedings on the claim are maintainable under s 182` f MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `notice is in order` AND `the claim is in time`
            THEN `maintainable: notice given and the claim made within 6 months (s 182(1))`
        IF (NOT `notice is in order`) AND (NOT (f's `the employer is not prejudiced, or the want of notice had a reasonable cause`))
            THEN `not maintainable: no notice before the worker voluntarily left the employment, and s 182(2) does not excuse it`
        IF (NOT `the claim is in time`) AND (NOT `the late claim has a reasonable cause`)
            THEN `not maintainable: the claim was not made within 6 months and no reasonable cause is found (s 182(1), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `maintainable: the want of notice or the late claim is excused (s 182(2), (3), (5))`
    WHERE
        `notice is in order` MEANS f's `notice of the injury was given before the worker voluntarily left the employment`
        `the last day to claim` MEANS `the last day to claim under s 182(1)` (f's `the day of the injury, or of the incapacity from a disease, or the day advice of the death was received`)
        `the claim is in time` MEANS ((f's `the day the claim was made`) `is on or before` `the last day to claim`)
        `the late claim has a reasonable cause` MEANS
                f's `the late claim is found to have been occasioned by mistake, ignorance of a disease, absence from the Territory or other reasonable cause`
            OR  f's `the claimant was paid what they believed was compensation, or the employer or insurer led them to believe compensation would be paid or was not available`

-- s 82(2): "If the claim and medical certificate of capacity are not given or served at the same time, the remaining
-- document shall be given or served on the employer within 28 days after the first document is given or served and the
-- claim for compensation shall be deemed not to have been made until the day on which the remaining document is given".
-- s 82(1)(b): no certificate is needed for a claim under s 62, 63 or 73 (death, children's benefits, medical costs).
-- s 82(6) with reg 12(2): a certificate "has effect" for 14 days (from a medical practitioner, or a prescribed nurse or
-- Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health practitioner who consulted one), or 3 days (from one who did not).
`the s 82(2) period, in days` MEANS 28

GIVEN `the day the first document was given` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to give the remaining claim document under s 82(2)` `the day the first document was given` MEANS
    `the day the first document was given` `plus days` `the s 82(2) period, in days`

DECLARE `Certificate issuer` IS ONE OF
    `a medical practitioner`
    `a prescribed person who consulted a medical practitioner`
    `a prescribed person who did not consult a medical practitioner`

GIVEN `the issuer` IS A `Certificate issuer`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the days a medical certificate of capacity has effect under reg 12(2)` `the issuer` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the issuer`
    WHEN `a medical practitioner`                                     THEN 14
    WHEN `a prescribed person who consulted a medical practitioner`     THEN 14
    WHEN `a prescribed person who did not consult a medical practitioner` THEN 3

-- READING: the certificate has effect for the stated number of days counted from the day it was issued.
GIVEN `the issuer` IS A `Certificate issuer`, `the day it was issued` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day a certificate has effect` `the issuer` `the day it was issued` MEANS
    `the day it was issued` `plus days` (`the days a medical certificate of capacity has effect under reg 12(2)` `the issuer`)

§§ `s 49A -- normal weekly earnings`

-- s 49A(2): normal weekly earnings "are the gross remuneration paid to the worker by: (a) the employer liable to
-- compensate the worker; and (b) any other employer for whom the worker ordinarily works" (6: at least 6 weeks with that
-- employer and employed on the first compensation date). (3) includes: regular and established overtime; over-award
-- payments; climate, district, leading hand, qualification, regular shift allowances and service grants; and "unless the
-- worker is a fly-in fly-out worker or drive-in drive-out worker -- the value (not exceeding, in the aggregate, 35% of
-- average weekly earnings) of any electricity, meals or accommodation provided ... in a form other than an amount of money".
-- (4) excludes superannuation, other overtime, other allowances and other non-cash remuneration. (5): an unvarying weekly
-- amount, or the 12-month average where it varied, or the all-employer average for a new worker; (5)(e) the method giving
-- the greatest amount where in doubt. Those averages are payroll facts: the remuneration inputs are already worked out
-- under (5).
DECLARE `Earnings facts` HAS
    `weekly ordinary remuneration from the employer liable to compensate`               IS A NUMBER
    `weekly ordinary remuneration from other employers the worker ordinarily works for` IS A NUMBER
    `weekly wages for overtime worked in a regular and established pattern`             IS A NUMBER
    `weekly over-award payments`                                                        IS A NUMBER
    `weekly climate, district, leading hand, qualification or regular shift allowances and service grants` IS A NUMBER
    `weekly value of electricity, meals or accommodation provided other than in money`  IS A NUMBER
    `is a fly-in fly-out or drive-in drive-out worker`                                  IS A BOOLEAN

`the s 49A(3)(d) cap, as a share of average weekly earnings` MEANS 35%

GIVEN e IS AN `Earnings facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the normal weekly earnings under s 49A` e `average weekly earnings` MEANS
        e's `weekly ordinary remuneration from the employer liable to compensate`
    PLUS e's `weekly ordinary remuneration from other employers the worker ordinarily works for`
    PLUS e's `weekly wages for overtime worked in a regular and established pattern`
    PLUS e's `weekly over-award payments`
    PLUS e's `weekly climate, district, leading hand, qualification or regular shift allowances and service grants`
    PLUS `the counted value in kind`
    WHERE
        `the counted value in kind` MEANS
            IF e's `is a fly-in fly-out or drive-in drive-out worker`
            THEN 0
            ELSE `the lesser amount of` (e's `weekly value of electricity, meals or accommodation provided other than in money`) `and` (`the s 49A(3)(d) cap, as a share of average weekly earnings` TIMES `average weekly earnings`)

§§ `s 64 -- the first 26 weeks`

-- s 64(3): "The weekly amount of the compensation is calculated as the difference between the following amounts: (a) the
-- amount that the worker actually earned in employment during the week; (b) the worker's normal weekly earnings
-- immediately before the first compensation date." (5): at most 26 weeks in total, each certified by a medical practitioner,
-- with actual economic loss. (5A): a part week counts as a week. READING: no compensation where the worker earned at least
-- the normal weekly earnings (no economic loss).
`the s 64 weeks` MEANS 26

GIVEN `normal weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER, `the amount actually earned in the week` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 64 weekly amount` `normal weekly earnings` `the amount actually earned in the week` MEANS
    `the greater amount of` (`normal weekly earnings` MINUS `the amount actually earned in the week`) `and` 0

§§ `s 65 -- long-term incapacity: the amount`

-- s 65(1B): "compensation ... equal to whichever of the following is the lesser at the time the payment is made: (a) 75% of
-- the worker's loss of earning capacity; (b) 150% of average weekly earnings." (1C): only after the first 26 weeks.
-- (2): "loss of earning capacity ... is the difference between: (a) his or her normal weekly earnings indexed in accordance
-- with subsection (3); and (b) the amount, if any, he or she is from time to time reasonably capable of earning in a week"
-- in the most profitable employment reasonably available (first 104 weeks) or that could be undertaken, available or not
-- (after 104 weeks), having regard to s 68. (6): totally incapacitated if "not capable of earning any amount".
-- (3), (3A): the indexed normal weekly earnings "are the amount calculated by using the formula in subsection (3A)". In the
-- deposited text (3A) reads "The formula for subsection (3)(a) is the following: where: N is ... the normal weekly earnings
-- ... immediately before the first compensation date; A is the amount of average weekly earnings at the particular date ...;
-- C is the amount of average weekly earnings applying at the first compensation date." The formula itself did not survive
-- conversion. The indexed amount is an INPUT; `the indexed normal weekly earnings on the flagged reading N x A / C` below is
-- a READING, not the text.
`the s 65(1B)(a) share of loss of earning capacity`        MEANS 75%
`the s 65(1B)(b) cap, as a share of average weekly earnings` MEANS 150%
`the s 65(7), (8) share of loss of earning capacity`        MEANS 90%
`the s 65(10) spouse's earnings, as a share of average weekly earnings` MEANS 150%
`the s 65(12) base share of average weekly earnings`          MEANS 50%
`the s 65(12)(a) share for a dependent spouse`                MEANS 12.5%
`the s 65(12)(b) share for each prescribed child`              MEANS 6.25%

-- FLAGGED READING of s 65(3A): N x A / C. Not in the deposited text; see NOTES.md finding 1.
GIVEN `N, the normal weekly earnings immediately before the first compensation date` IS A NUMBER
      `A, average weekly earnings at the particular date` IS A NUMBER
      `C, average weekly earnings applying at the first compensation date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the indexed normal weekly earnings on the flagged reading N x A / C` `N, the normal weekly earnings immediately before the first compensation date` `A, average weekly earnings at the particular date` `C, average weekly earnings applying at the first compensation date` MEANS
    (`N, the normal weekly earnings immediately before the first compensation date` TIMES `A, average weekly earnings at the particular date`) DIVIDED BY `C, average weekly earnings applying at the first compensation date`

DECLARE `Long-term incapacity facts` HAS
    `indexed normal weekly earnings`                                   IS A NUMBER
    `the amount the worker is reasonably capable of earning in a week`  IS A NUMBER
    `the worker's spouse is wholly or mainly dependent on the worker`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `the number of the worker's prescribed children`                    IS A NUMBER
    `the worker's spouse's normal weekly earnings`                      IS A NUMBER

GIVEN l IS A `Long-term incapacity facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the loss of earning capacity under s 65(2)` l MEANS
    `the greater amount of` (l's `indexed normal weekly earnings` MINUS l's `the amount the worker is reasonably capable of earning in a week`) `and` 0

GIVEN l IS A `Long-term incapacity facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the worker is totally incapacitated under s 65(6)` l IF
    l's `the amount the worker is reasonably capable of earning in a week` AT MOST 0

GIVEN l IS A `Long-term incapacity facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 65(1B) amount` l `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    `the lesser amount of` (`the s 65(1B)(a) share of loss of earning capacity` TIMES `the loss of earning capacity under s 65(2)` l) `and` (`the s 65(1B)(b) cap, as a share of average weekly earnings` TIMES `average weekly earnings`)

-- s 65(12): "the amount is 50% of average weekly earnings and, in addition: (a) 12.5% of average weekly earnings, where the
-- worker has a spouse who is, at the date of the relevant injury and at the time when compensation is paid under this
-- section, wholly or mainly dependent on the worker; and (b) 6.25% of average weekly earnings for each prescribed child of
-- the worker."
GIVEN l IS A `Long-term incapacity facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 65(12) amount` l `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    `average weekly earnings` TIMES (`the s 65(12) base share of average weekly earnings` PLUS `the spouse share` PLUS `the children share`)
    WHERE
        `the spouse share` MEANS
            IF l's `the worker's spouse is wholly or mainly dependent on the worker` THEN `the s 65(12)(a) share for a dependent spouse` ELSE 0
        `the children share` MEANS `the s 65(12)(b) share for each prescribed child` TIMES l's `the number of the worker's prescribed children`

-- s 65(10): "Subsections (7) and (8) do not apply where the normal weekly earnings of the spouse of the worker is equal to
-- or more than 150% of average weekly earnings."
GIVEN l IS A `Long-term incapacity facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 65(10) shuts out the low-earner floor` l `average weekly earnings` IF
    l's `the worker's spouse's normal weekly earnings` AT LEAST (`the s 65(10) spouse's earnings, as a share of average weekly earnings` TIMES `average weekly earnings`)

-- s 65(7): totally incapacitated, and the (1B) amount "is less than the amount calculated in accordance with subsection
-- (12)": "90% of his or her loss of earning capacity or the amount so calculated, whichever is the lesser".
-- s 65(8): partially incapacitated, and "75% of his or her normal weekly earnings indexed in accordance with subsection (3)
-- is less than the amount calculated in accordance with subsection (12)": "90% of his or her loss of earning capacity or the
-- percentage of his or her loss of earning capacity as determined in accordance with subsection (9), whichever is the
-- lesser". (9): "the percentage is the amount calculated in accordance with subsection (12) expressed as a percentage of
-- the worker's normal weekly earnings" (indexed, by s 65(3)).
GIVEN l IS A `Long-term incapacity facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 65 weekly amount` l `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the s 65(7) floor applies`
            THEN `the lesser amount of` (`the s 65(7), (8) share of loss of earning capacity` TIMES `the loss`) `and` `the (12) amount`
        IF `the s 65(8) floor applies`
            THEN `the lesser amount of` (`the s 65(7), (8) share of loss of earning capacity` TIMES `the loss`) `and` ((`the (12) amount` DIVIDED BY l's `indexed normal weekly earnings`) TIMES `the loss`)
        OTHERWISE `the s 65(1B) amount` l `average weekly earnings`
    WHERE
        `the loss`         MEANS `the loss of earning capacity under s 65(2)` l
        `the (12) amount`  MEANS `the s 65(12) amount` l `average weekly earnings`
        `the s 65(7) floor applies` MEANS
                `the worker is totally incapacitated under s 65(6)` l
            AND (`the s 65(1B) amount` l `average weekly earnings`) LESS THAN `the (12) amount`
            AND (NOT (`s 65(10) shuts out the low-earner floor` l `average weekly earnings`))
        `the s 65(8) floor applies` MEANS
                (NOT (`the worker is totally incapacitated under s 65(6)` l))
            AND l's `indexed normal weekly earnings` GREATER THAN 0
            AND (`the s 65(1B)(a) share of loss of earning capacity` TIMES l's `indexed normal weekly earnings`) LESS THAN `the (12) amount`
            AND (NOT (`s 65(10) shuts out the low-earner floor` l `average weekly earnings`))

§§ `ss 61A, 64, 65(1BA), 65A, 65B -- this week's weekly payment`

-- s 65(1BA): for a worker whose relevant permanent impairment is under 15%, s 65 compensation is payable "for a period that
-- does not exceed a period of 260 weeks in the aggregate (which includes the first 26 weeks for which compensation is
-- payable under section 64)". s 61A(2)(a)(i): after 260 weeks, "no other compensation under section 65 is payable".
-- s 65(13B), s 61A(2A): a part week counts as a week.
-- s 65A(1): no compensation under s 64 or 65 "during any period during which the worker is: (a) a prisoner; or (b)" detained,
-- or in like circumstances, outside the Territory; (2), (3): unless no one is liable to maintain the worker (asked as one
-- fact). s 65B(1): the entitlement "is suspended when the worker begins to reside outside Australia unless the entitlement
-- continues"; (2): it continues if "(a) the worker complies with the requirements prescribed by regulation" (reg 6A: "at
-- intervals of not less than 3 months, a declaration" by the worker and a medical practitioner) "and (b) the employer is
-- satisfied about the worker's continued incapacity"; (3): it "ceases when, in aggregate, the worker is paid compensation
-- because of the application or applications of subsection (2) for 104 weeks (the period of 104 weeks includes the first
-- 26 weeks referred to in section 64)"; (4)-(7): the Court may extend it by up to 104 weeks. READING of (3), the one
-- closest to its words (NOTES.md finding 19): the weeks counted are the weeks paid "because of the application ... of
-- subsection (2)", that is, paid while the worker lived outside Australia, together with the first 26 weeks, which the
-- parenthesis counts wherever they were paid. Weeks after the 26th that were paid while the worker lived in Australia
-- are not counted. (The first version of this encoding counted every week of weekly payments.)
`the s 65(1BA) weeks` MEANS 260
`the s 65B(3) weeks`  MEANS 104

DECLARE `Week facts` HAS
    `the weeks of weekly payments already paid for the injury`                         IS A NUMBER
    `a medical practitioner certifies the worker incapacitated for the week`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `the worker's relevant permanent impairment is 15% or more`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `normal weekly earnings`                                                           IS A NUMBER
    `the amount the worker actually earned in employment during the week`              IS A NUMBER
    `the worker is a prisoner, or detained outside the Territory, and someone is liable to maintain the worker` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the worker lives outside Australia`                                               IS A BOOLEAN
    `the worker gives the reg 6A declarations`                                         IS A BOOLEAN
    `the employer is satisfied about the worker's continued incapacity`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `a Court order under s 65B(4) extends the entitlement for the week`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `the weeks after the first 26 already paid while the worker lived outside Australia` IS A NUMBER
    `long-term facts`                                                                  IS A `Long-term incapacity facts`

DECLARE `Weekly compensation answer` IS ONE OF
    `s 64 compensation, in dollars for the week` HAS `dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `s 65 compensation, in dollars for the week` HAS `dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `none: the week is not certified by a medical practitioner (s 64(2), (5)(b))`
    `none: the 260 weeks have been paid (ss 65(1BA), 61A(2)(a))`
    `none: suspended while a prisoner (s 65A)`
    `none: suspended while living outside Australia (s 65B)`

GIVEN w IS A `Week facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the s 65B entitlement continues outside Australia` w IF
        w's `the worker gives the reg 6A declarations`
    AND w's `the employer is satisfied about the worker's continued incapacity`
    AND (   `the weeks counted under s 65B(3)` w LESS THAN `the s 65B(3) weeks`
         OR w's `a Court order under s 65B(4) extends the entitlement for the week`)

-- s 65B(3), on the READING above: the first 26 weeks (or as many of them as have been paid), and the weeks after them that
-- were paid while the worker lived outside Australia.
GIVEN w IS A `Week facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the weeks counted under s 65B(3)` w MEANS
        (`the smaller number of` (w's `the weeks of weekly payments already paid for the injury`) `and` `the s 64 weeks`)
    PLUS w's `the weeks after the first 26 already paid while the worker lived outside Australia`

GIVEN w IS A `Week facts`, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Weekly compensation answer`
`the weekly compensation for the week` w `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `this is one of the first 26 weeks` AND (NOT (w's `a medical practitioner certifies the worker incapacitated for the week`))
            THEN `none: the week is not certified by a medical practitioner (s 64(2), (5)(b))`
        IF (NOT (w's `the worker's relevant permanent impairment is 15% or more`)) AND w's `the weeks of weekly payments already paid for the injury` AT LEAST `the s 65(1BA) weeks`
            THEN `none: the 260 weeks have been paid (ss 65(1BA), 61A(2)(a))`
        IF w's `the worker is a prisoner, or detained outside the Territory, and someone is liable to maintain the worker`
            THEN `none: suspended while a prisoner (s 65A)`
        IF w's `the worker lives outside Australia` AND (NOT (`the s 65B entitlement continues outside Australia` w))
            THEN `none: suspended while living outside Australia (s 65B)`
        IF `this is one of the first 26 weeks`
            THEN `s 64 compensation, in dollars for the week` (`the s 64 weekly amount` (w's `normal weekly earnings`) (w's `the amount the worker actually earned in employment during the week`))
        OTHERWISE `s 65 compensation, in dollars for the week` (`the s 65 weekly amount` (w's `long-term facts`) `average weekly earnings`)
    WHERE
        `this is one of the first 26 weeks` MEANS w's `the weeks of weekly payments already paid for the injury` LESS THAN `the s 64 weeks`

§§ `ss 65(1D), (14), 61A -- until when weekly payments are paid`

-- s 65(1D): "(a) if the worker's injury date is 2 years or more before the date on which the worker attains the worker's
-- future pension age and the worker suffers relevant permanent impairment at a percentage of the whole person equal to not
-- less than 15% -- the compensation is to be paid: (i) if the worker's normal retiring age is later than the worker's future
-- pension age -- until the worker attains the worker's normal retiring age; or (ii) otherwise -- until the worker attains the
-- worker's future pension age; (b) if [the same, but] less than 15% -- ... until the earlier of the following: (A) the end
-- of the last week in the period of 260 weeks in the aggregate ...; (B) the date on which the worker attains the worker's
-- normal retiring age [or, (ii), future pension age]; or (c) the compensation is to be paid for 104 weeks from the injury
-- date if: (i) the worker's normal retiring age is later than the worker's future pension age and the injury date is less
-- than 2 years before the date on which the worker attains the worker's normal retiring age; or (ii) [that, and] the injury
-- date is on or after the date on which the worker attains the worker's normal retiring age; or (iii) the worker's normal
-- retiring age is not later than the worker's future pension age and the injury date is less than 2 years before the date
-- on which the worker attains the worker's future pension age; or (iv) [that, and on or after it]."
-- s 65(14): "future pension age ... means the age at which (once attained) the worker would ... be eligible to receive an
-- age pension under the Social Security Act 1991 (Cth)" (an INPUT here, in months: the Commonwealth encoding's
-- `What is my pension age in months` answers it); "normal retiring age ... means the age that workers in the industry or
-- occupation in which the worker is employed usually retire" (an INPUT, in months).
-- GAP (NOTES.md finding 3): where normal retiring age is later than future pension age, and the injury date is less than
-- 2 years before future pension age but 2 years or more before normal retiring age, no paragraph of (1D) applies. The
-- answer is `not covered`.
-- READING: "for 104 weeks from the injury date" ends 104 weeks (728 days) after the injury date.
`the s 65(1D) two years, in months` MEANS 24
`the s 65(1D)(c) weeks`             MEANS 104

DECLARE `Period facts` HAS
    `the worker's date of birth`                                          IS A DATE
    `the injury date`                                                     IS A DATE
    `the worker's future pension age, in months`                          IS A NUMBER
    `the normal retiring age in the worker's industry or occupation, in months` IS A NUMBER
    `the worker's relevant permanent impairment is 15% or more`           IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Payment period answer` IS ONE OF
    `until the worker attains normal retiring age, on` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `until the worker attains future pension age, on` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `until 260 weeks have been paid, or normal retiring age on this day if that is earlier` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `until 260 weeks have been paid, or future pension age on this day if that is earlier` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `for 104 weeks from the injury date, to` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `not covered: s 65(1D) states no period for an injury less than 2 years before future pension age but 2 years or more before a later normal retiring age`

GIVEN p IS A `Period facts`
GIVETH A `Payment period answer`
`the period of s 65 compensation under s 65(1D)` p MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `two years or more before` `the pension day` AND p's `the worker's relevant permanent impairment is 15% or more` AND `retiring age is later`
            THEN `until the worker attains normal retiring age, on` `the retiring day`
        IF `two years or more before` `the pension day` AND p's `the worker's relevant permanent impairment is 15% or more`
            THEN `until the worker attains future pension age, on` `the pension day`
        IF `two years or more before` `the pension day` AND `retiring age is later`
            THEN `until 260 weeks have been paid, or normal retiring age on this day if that is earlier` `the retiring day`
        IF `two years or more before` `the pension day`
            THEN `until 260 weeks have been paid, or future pension age on this day if that is earlier` `the pension day`
        IF `retiring age is later` AND (NOT (`two years or more before` `the retiring day`))
            THEN `for 104 weeks from the injury date, to` `the day 104 weeks after the injury`
        IF (NOT `retiring age is later`)
            THEN `for 104 weeks from the injury date, to` `the day 104 weeks after the injury`
        OTHERWISE `not covered: s 65(1D) states no period for an injury less than 2 years before future pension age but 2 years or more before a later normal retiring age`
    WHERE
        `the pension day`  MEANS `the day a person born on` (p's `the worker's date of birth`) `attains an age in months of` (p's `the worker's future pension age, in months`)
        `the retiring day` MEANS `the day a person born on` (p's `the worker's date of birth`) `attains an age in months of` (p's `the normal retiring age in the worker's industry or occupation, in months`)
        `retiring age is later` MEANS
            p's `the normal retiring age in the worker's industry or occupation, in months` GREATER THAN p's `the worker's future pension age, in months`
        `two years or more before` `the day` MEANS
            (((p's `the injury date`) `plus months` `the s 65(1D) two years, in months`) `is on or before` `the day`)
        `the day 104 weeks after the injury` MEANS (p's `the injury date`) `plus days` (`the s 65(1D)(c) weeks` TIMES 7)

-- s 61A(2)(a)(ii): after 260 weeks of s 65 compensation, "no other compensation under this Part, other than under section 71,
-- is payable to the worker in respect of the injury for a cost that was incurred after 12 months after the end of the last
-- of the 260 weeks". s 61A(2)(b)(ii), for a shorter period under s 65(1D), counts 12 months from "the end of the last of
-- the relevant number of weeks", and (3) defines that number as 260 less the shorter period without saying where it starts
-- (NOTES.md finding 11): not encoded.
`the s 61A(2)(a)(ii) months` MEANS 12

GIVEN `the end of the last of the 260 weeks` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day a cost may be incurred under s 61A(2)(a)(ii)` `the end of the last of the 260 weeks` MEANS
    `the end of the last of the 260 weeks` `plus months` `the s 61A(2)(a)(ii) months`

-- s 61B(4): the change from a statutory event (the end of the s 64 weeks, of the 260 weeks, attaining normal retiring age
-- or future pension age, the end of the 104 weeks in s 65(1D)(c)) "takes effect on the later of the following days: (a)
-- the day that is 14 days after the employer gives the notice to the worker; (b) the day on which the statutory event
-- occurs." s 61B(3)(c): a posted notice "is considered to have been given when the envelope is posted".
`the s 61B(4)(a) days` MEANS 14

GIVEN `the day the notice was given or posted` IS A DATE, `the day the statutory event occurs` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day the change takes effect under s 61B(4)` `the day the notice was given or posted` `the day the statutory event occurs` MEANS
    `the later of` (`the day the notice was given or posted` `plus days` `the s 61B(4)(a) days`) `and` `the day the statutory event occurs`

§§ `ss 70, 71, 72 -- compensation for permanent impairment`

-- s 70: "permanent impairment means an impairment ... assessed, in accordance with the guides ..., as being an impairment
-- ... of not less than 5% of the whole person." s 71(1): at 15% or more, "compensation equal to that assessed percentage of
-- 208 times average weekly earnings at the time the payment is made"; (2): at 85% or more, "208 times average weekly
-- earnings"; (3): below 15%, "the percentage specified in column 2 of the Table ... of 208 times average weekly earnings".
-- The Table: not less than 5% but less than 10%: 2; 10%: 3; 11%: 4; 12%: 6; 13%: 8; 14%: 12.
-- The Table lists only whole percentages from 10% to 14%: an assessment between them (10.5%) has no row, and answers
-- `not covered` (NOTES.md finding 5). The percentage is given as a number (14 for 14%).
`the s 71 multiple of average weekly earnings` MEANS 208

DECLARE `Impairment lump sum answer` IS ONE OF
    `a lump sum, in dollars, of` HAS `dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `none: less than 5% of the whole person is not a permanent impairment (s 70)`
    `not covered: the Table to s 71(3) has no row for a percentage from 10% to 15% that is not a whole number`

GIVEN `the assessed percentage` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the percentage of 208 times average weekly earnings under s 71` `the assessed percentage` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the assessed percentage` AT LEAST 85 THEN JUST 100
        IF `the assessed percentage` AT LEAST 15 THEN JUST `the assessed percentage`
        IF `the assessed percentage` AT LEAST 10 AND (NOT (FLOOR `the assessed percentage` EQUALS `the assessed percentage`)) THEN NOTHING
        IF `the assessed percentage` EQUALS 14 THEN JUST 12
        IF `the assessed percentage` EQUALS 13 THEN JUST 8
        IF `the assessed percentage` EQUALS 12 THEN JUST 6
        IF `the assessed percentage` EQUALS 11 THEN JUST 4
        IF `the assessed percentage` EQUALS 10 THEN JUST 3
        IF `the assessed percentage` AT LEAST 5 THEN JUST 2
        OTHERWISE JUST 0

GIVEN `the assessed percentage` IS A NUMBER, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN `Impairment lump sum answer`
`the permanent impairment compensation under s 71` `the assessed percentage` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF `the assessed percentage` LESS THAN 5
    THEN `none: less than 5% of the whole person is not a permanent impairment (s 70)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the percentage of 208 times average weekly earnings under s 71` `the assessed percentage`
         WHEN JUST pc THEN `a lump sum, in dollars, of` ((pc DIVIDED BY 100) TIMES `the s 71 multiple of average weekly earnings` TIMES `average weekly earnings`)
         WHEN NOTHING THEN `not covered: the Table to s 71(3) has no row for a percentage from 10% to 15% that is not a whole number`

-- s 72(3): "Where a person is aggrieved by the assessment of the level of permanent impairment by a medical practitioner,
-- the person may, within 28 days after being notified of the assessment, apply to the Authority for a reassessment".
-- s 71(4): payment "(a) if no application is made under section 72(3) ... -- not later than 14 days after the end of the 28
-- day period allowed for that application; or (b) if an application is made ... -- not later than 28 days after the
-- applicant is notified of the reassessment." s 72(5): the employer pays for the assessment and reassessment.
`the s 72(3) days`    MEANS 28
`the s 71(4)(a) days` MEANS 14
`the s 71(4)(b) days` MEANS 28

GIVEN `the day the worker was notified of the assessment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to apply for a reassessment under s 72(3)` `the day the worker was notified of the assessment` MEANS
    `the day the worker was notified of the assessment` `plus days` `the s 72(3) days`

GIVEN `the day the worker was notified of the assessment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to pay under s 71(4)(a)` `the day the worker was notified of the assessment` MEANS
    (`the last day to apply for a reassessment under s 72(3)` `the day the worker was notified of the assessment`) `plus days` `the s 71(4)(a) days`

GIVEN `the day the applicant was notified of the reassessment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to pay under s 71(4)(b)` `the day the applicant was notified of the reassessment` MEANS
    `the day the applicant was notified of the reassessment` `plus days` `the s 71(4)(b) days`

-- s 71(4): which of (a) and (b) applies turns on whether an application for a reassessment is made under s 72(3).
GIVEN `an application for a reassessment was made under s 72(3)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the worker was notified of the assessment` IS A DATE
      `the day the applicant was notified of the reassessment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to pay the permanent impairment compensation under s 71(4)` `an application for a reassessment was made under s 72(3)` `the day the worker was notified of the assessment` `the day the applicant was notified of the reassessment` MEANS
    IF `an application for a reassessment was made under s 72(3)`
    THEN `the last day to pay under s 71(4)(b)` `the day the applicant was notified of the reassessment`
    ELSE `the last day to pay under s 71(4)(a)` `the day the worker was notified of the assessment`

§§ `ss 62, 63 and regs 6, 6AA -- death`

-- s 62(1)(a): "to the person liable to meet the expense of the worker's funeral, a funeral benefit equal to: (i) the cost of
-- the funeral; or (ii) the prescribed amount, whichever is the lesser amount". Reg 6: "the prescribed amount is 20% of the
-- annual equivalent of average weekly earnings".
`the reg 6 share of the annual equivalent, for the funeral benefit` MEANS 20%

GIVEN `the cost of the funeral` IS A NUMBER, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the funeral benefit under s 62(1)(a)` `the cost of the funeral` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    `the lesser amount of` `the cost of the funeral` `and` (`the reg 6 share of the annual equivalent, for the funeral benefit` TIMES `the annual equivalent of` `average weekly earnings`)

-- s 62(1)(b): "for the benefit of the worker's dependants: (i) where the dependants are a spouse, a child or children, or a
-- spouse and a child or children, the prescribed proportions; or (ii) such proportions as the Court determines ... of an
-- amount equal to 364 times average weekly earnings at the time the payment is made". (3) Table: Spouse 100%; one child
-- 100%; 2 or more children equally between children; spouse and one child 10% to child, balance to spouse; spouse and not
-- more than 5 children 5% to each child, balance to spouse; spouse and more than 5 children 25% divided equally between
-- children, balance to spouse. s 49(1): a dependant is a spouse (including a de facto partner), family member, person in
-- loco parentis or grandchild "wholly or in part dependent" on the worker's earnings. READING: the Table applies only where
-- every dependant is the spouse or a child; any other dependant sends the whole to the Court under (1)(b)(ii).
`the s 62 multiple of average weekly earnings` MEANS 364

DECLARE `Dependants` HAS
    `there is a dependant spouse`              IS A BOOLEAN
    `the number of dependant children`         IS A NUMBER
    `there are other dependants`               IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Death share answer` IS ONE OF
    `a share, in dollars, of` HAS `dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `as the Court determines on an application under s 62(2)`
    `no share: there is no dependant of that kind`

GIVEN `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 62(1)(b) amount` `average weekly earnings` MEANS `the s 62 multiple of average weekly earnings` TIMES `average weekly earnings`

GIVEN d IS A `Dependants`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`the spouse's proportion under the s 62(3) Table` d MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF d's `there are other dependants` THEN NOTHING
        IF NOT (d's `there is a dependant spouse`) THEN JUST 0
        IF d's `the number of dependant children` EQUALS 0 THEN JUST (100%)
        IF d's `the number of dependant children` EQUALS 1 THEN JUST (90%)
        IF d's `the number of dependant children` AT MOST 5 THEN JUST (100% MINUS (5% TIMES d's `the number of dependant children`))
        OTHERWISE JUST (75%)

GIVEN d IS A `Dependants`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`each child's proportion under the s 62(3) Table` d MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF d's `there are other dependants` THEN NOTHING
        IF d's `the number of dependant children` EQUALS 0 THEN JUST 0
        IF NOT (d's `there is a dependant spouse`) THEN JUST (100% DIVIDED BY d's `the number of dependant children`)
        IF d's `the number of dependant children` EQUALS 1 THEN JUST (10%)
        IF d's `the number of dependant children` AT MOST 5 THEN JUST (5%)
        OTHERWISE JUST (25% DIVIDED BY d's `the number of dependant children`)

GIVEN `the proportion` IS A MAYBE NUMBER, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Death share answer`
`the death share from` `the proportion` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the proportion`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `as the Court determines on an application under s 62(2)`
    WHEN JUST pr THEN
        IF pr AT MOST 0
        THEN `no share: there is no dependant of that kind`
        ELSE `a share, in dollars, of` (pr TIMES `the s 62(1)(b) amount` `average weekly earnings`)

-- s 62(1)(c): "the reasonable costs (not exceeding, in the aggregate, any amount prescribed by regulation) of one or both
-- of the following: (i) counselling services provided to them by a counselling professional; (ii) expert financial advice."
-- Reg 6AA defines expert financial advice (a service needing an Australian financial services licence) but prescribes no
-- amount, so the deposited Regulations set no cap: the cap is NOTHING unless one is given.
GIVEN `the reasonable costs` IS A NUMBER, `the prescribed cap` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the counselling and financial advice costs payable under s 62(1)(c)` `the reasonable costs` `the prescribed cap` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the prescribed cap`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `the reasonable costs`
    WHEN JUST cap THEN `the lesser amount of` `the reasonable costs` `and` cap

-- s 62(4): a child's share is paid, "where the child has not attained the age of 18 years, to the Public Trustee", on
-- trust until 18, "Except where the Court orders otherwise".
GIVEN `the child's age in years` IS A NUMBER, `the Court has ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the child's share is paid to the Public Trustee under s 62(4)` `the child's age in years` `the Court has ordered otherwise` IF
    `the child's age in years` LESS THAN 18 AND (NOT `the Court has ordered otherwise`)

-- s 63(3), and s 65(13) in the same words: "prescribed child means a child of the deceased worker [of the worker], or a
-- child in relation to whom the [deceased] worker stood in loco parentis, and who: (a) has not attained the age of 16 years;
-- or (b) having attained that age but not having attained the age of 21 years, is a full-time student or is physically or
-- mentally handicapped, other than such a child who is the spouse of another person."
DECLARE `Child facts` HAS
    `is a child of the worker or one the worker stood in loco parentis to` IS A BOOLEAN
    `age in years`                                                       IS A NUMBER
    `is a full-time student, or physically or mentally handicapped`       IS A BOOLEAN
    `is the spouse of another person`                                    IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN k IS A `Child facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the child is a prescribed child under ss 63(3) and 65(13)` k IF
        k's `is a child of the worker or one the worker stood in loco parentis to`
    AND (   k's `age in years` LESS THAN 16
         OR (k's `age in years` LESS THAN 21 AND k's `is a full-time student, or physically or mentally handicapped`))
    AND (NOT (k's `is the spouse of another person`))

-- s 63(1): "an amount per week equal to 10% of average weekly earnings at the time the payment is made" for each prescribed
-- child; (2): the total "shall not exceed 100% of average weekly earnings and where there are more than 10 prescribed
-- children ... they shall be entitled in equal shares to an amount equal to 100% of average weekly earnings".
`the s 63(1) share of average weekly earnings, for each child` MEANS 10%
`the s 63(2) cap, as a share of average weekly earnings`       MEANS 100%

GIVEN `the number of prescribed children` IS A NUMBER, `average weekly earnings` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the weekly benefit for each prescribed child under s 63` `the number of prescribed children` `average weekly earnings` MEANS
    IF `the number of prescribed children` AT MOST 0
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF (`the s 63(1) share of average weekly earnings, for each child` TIMES `the number of prescribed children`) AT MOST `the s 63(2) cap, as a share of average weekly earnings`
         THEN `the s 63(1) share of average weekly earnings, for each child` TIMES `average weekly earnings`
         ELSE (`the s 63(2) cap, as a share of average weekly earnings` TIMES `average weekly earnings`) DIVIDED BY `the number of prescribed children`

§§ `s 74 -- recovering an overpayment from the worker`

-- s 74(2): "Unless otherwise ordered by the Court, an overpayment cannot be recovered from the worker to whom the
-- overpayment was made if: (a) the benefit payable was incorrectly calculated by the employer or insurer who made the
-- payment; or (b) the payment was made in respect of a period more than 6 months before the date on which recovery of the
-- overpaid amount was sought." (3): the Court weighs whether the worker should reasonably have known. READING of (b): the
-- period ended more than 6 months before the day recovery was sought.
`the s 74(2)(b) months` MEANS 6

DECLARE `Overpayment answer` IS ONE OF
    `the overpayment may be recovered from the worker`
    `not recoverable unless the Court orders otherwise: the employer or insurer calculated the benefit incorrectly (s 74(2)(a))`
    `not recoverable unless the Court orders otherwise: it was paid for a period more than 6 months before recovery was sought (s 74(2)(b))`

GIVEN `the payer calculated the benefit incorrectly` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the last day of the period the payment was for` IS A DATE
      `the day recovery was sought` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Overpayment answer`
`whether the overpayment can be recovered under s 74` `the payer calculated the benefit incorrectly` `the last day of the period the payment was for` `the day recovery was sought` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the payer calculated the benefit incorrectly`
            THEN `not recoverable unless the Court orders otherwise: the employer or insurer calculated the benefit incorrectly (s 74(2)(a))`
        IF NOT ((`the day recovery was sought` `plus months` (0 MINUS `the s 74(2)(b) months`)) `is on or before` `the last day of the period the payment was for`)
            THEN `not recoverable unless the Court orders otherwise: it was paid for a period more than 6 months before recovery was sought (s 74(2)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `the overpayment may be recovered from the worker`

§§ `ss 84, 85, 85A, 86, 87, 88, 89 -- the employer's deadlines on a claim`

-- s 84(1): "within 3 working days after receiving the claim", forward it (to the insurer; if self-insured, a copy to the
-- Authority; if uninsured, to the Nominal Insurer and a copy to the Authority). s 85(1): accept, defer or dispute "and shall
-- notify the person making the claim of the employer's decision within 10 working days after receiving the claim". (2): on
-- accepting a claim for weekly payments, "commence those payments within 3 working days after accepting liability". (4)(a):
-- a deferral "shall remain in force for 56 days from the date the notification under subsection (1) is given" (or longer if
-- the Court allows); (4)(b): weekly payments must start "within 3 working days of making the decision to defer". (6)(c):
-- notice by post "shall be deemed given when the envelope is posted". s 85A(2): earnings information "within 21 days after
-- receiving the request". s 86(2): an application to alter weekly payments is accepted or rejected "within 14 days", and (3)
-- payments at a new level start within that period. s 87(1)(a), (2)(a): deemed acceptance runs until "the expiry of 14 days
-- after the day on which the employer notifies" a decision. s 88(1): "before the expiration of 7 days after the end of the
-- week in respect of which it is payable" (or of a longer normal pay period), unless the worker agrees otherwise in writing.
`the s 84(1) working days`  MEANS 3
`the s 85(1) working days`  MEANS 10
`the s 85(2) working days`  MEANS 3
`the s 85(4)(a) days`       MEANS 56
`the s 85A(2) days`         MEANS 21
`the s 86(2) days`          MEANS 14
`the s 87 days`             MEANS 14
`the s 87(4)(a) working days` MEANS 10
`the s 88(1) days`          MEANS 7

GIVEN `the day the employer received the claim` IS A DATE, `weekday public holidays in the period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to forward the claim under s 84(1)` `the day the employer received the claim` `weekday public holidays in the period` MEANS
    `the last of` `the s 84(1) working days` `working days after` `the day the employer received the claim` `with public holidays` `weekday public holidays in the period`

-- s 84(1): "(a) where the employer is a self-insurer -- forward a copy of the claim to the Authority; and (ab) if the
-- employer is uninsured -- forward the claim to the Nominal Insurer and a copy of the claim to the Authority; and (b) in all
-- other cases -- forward the claim, and a copy, to his or her insurer; and (c) in all cases -- retain a copy".
DECLARE `Insurance position` IS ONE OF
    `insured with an approved insurer`
    `a self-insurer`
    `uninsured`

DECLARE `Forwarding answer` IS ONE OF
    `forward the claim, and a copy, to the insurer, and keep a copy (s 84(1)(b), (c))`
    `forward a copy of the claim to the Authority, and keep a copy (s 84(1)(a), (c))`
    `forward the claim to the Nominal Insurer and a copy to the Authority, and keep a copy (s 84(1)(ab), (c))`

GIVEN `the employer's position` IS AN `Insurance position`
GIVETH A `Forwarding answer`
`where the claim must be forwarded under s 84(1)` `the employer's position` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the employer's position`
    WHEN `insured with an approved insurer` THEN `forward the claim, and a copy, to the insurer, and keep a copy (s 84(1)(b), (c))`
    WHEN `a self-insurer`                   THEN `forward a copy of the claim to the Authority, and keep a copy (s 84(1)(a), (c))`
    WHEN `uninsured`                        THEN `forward the claim to the Nominal Insurer and a copy to the Authority, and keep a copy (s 84(1)(ab), (c))`

GIVEN `the day the employer received the claim` IS A DATE, `weekday public holidays in the period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to notify a decision under s 85(1)` `the day the employer received the claim` `weekday public holidays in the period` MEANS
    `the last of` `the s 85(1) working days` `working days after` `the day the employer received the claim` `with public holidays` `weekday public holidays in the period`

GIVEN `the day of the decision to accept or defer` IS A DATE, `weekday public holidays in the period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to commence weekly payments under s 85(2) or (4)(b)` `the day of the decision to accept or defer` `weekday public holidays in the period` MEANS
    `the last of` `the s 85(2) working days` `working days after` `the day of the decision to accept or defer` `with public holidays` `weekday public holidays in the period`

GIVEN `the day the deferral was notified or posted` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of a deferral under s 85(4)(a)` `the day the deferral was notified or posted` MEANS
    `the day the deferral was notified or posted` `plus days` `the s 85(4)(a) days`

GIVEN `the day the request was received` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to give earnings information under s 85A(2)` `the day the request was received` MEANS
    `the day the request was received` `plus days` `the s 85A(2) days`

GIVEN `the day the application was received` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to decide an application to alter weekly payments under s 86(2)` `the day the application was received` MEANS
    `the day the application was received` `plus days` `the s 86(2) days`

GIVEN `the last day of the week or normal pay period` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to make the weekly payment under s 88(1)` `the last day of the week or normal pay period` MEANS
    `the last day of the week or normal pay period` `plus days` `the s 88(1) days`

-- s 87: deemed acceptance. (1): "If an employer fails to notify a person of his or her decision within the time specified
-- in section 85(1), the employer is deemed to have accepted liability for compensation payable under Subdivisions B and D
-- of Division 3" (weekly payments; medical and other costs) "until: (a) the expiry of 14 days after the day on which the
-- employer notifies the person of his or her decision in pursuance of that section; or (b) the Court orders otherwise".
-- (2): the same where the employer "defers a decision on liability but fails to make a decision to accept or dispute
-- liability within the period for which the deferral remains in force under section 85(4)(a)", until 14 days after it
-- "notifies the claimant of a decision to accept or dispute liability". (3): "An employer notifies a claimant of a decision
-- for the purposes of subsection (1)(a) or (2)(a) if (and only if)" it is given as s 85(6) requires and, for a dispute,
-- with the s 85(8) statement whose reasons meet s 85(9). (4): an employer who fails to notify a decision mentioned in
-- (1)(a) or (2)(a) is considered to have accepted liability "effective on the day that is 10 working days after the
-- employer commences making payments".
--
-- Subsection (3) is confined to (1)(a) and (2)(a): it says which later notice ENDS a deemed acceptance. It does not govern
-- whether a decision was notified within the s 85(1) time, which is what (1) turns on, nor whether a deferral was ended by
-- a decision to accept or dispute under s 85(4)(a) and (2). So a dispute notified in time without the s 85(8) statement
-- still counts there: liability is not deemed accepted, and the employer has not met s 85(8) (NOTES.md finding 18). That
-- the notice was in writing and given as s 85(6) says is asked as a fact: "notified" below means notified in that way.
DECLARE `Liability decision` IS ONE OF
    `accept liability`
    `defer accepting liability`
    `dispute liability, with the s 85(8) statement`
    `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement`

DECLARE `Decision facts` HAS
    `the day the employer received the claim`                         IS A DATE
    `weekday public holidays in the 10 working days`                  IS A NUMBER
    `the employer has notified a decision`                            IS A BOOLEAN
    `the day the decision was notified or posted`                     IS A DATE
    `the decision`                                                    IS A `Liability decision`
    `after deferring, the employer has notified acceptance or a dispute` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the day that later decision was notified or posted`              IS A DATE
    `today`                                                           IS A DATE

DECLARE `Deemed acceptance answer` IS ONE OF
    `not deemed: the employer notified a decision in time`
    `not deemed: the employer notified a dispute in time, but without the s 85(8) statement, so it has not met s 85(8)`
    `not yet: the employer may still notify a decision, until` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `not yet: the deferral is in force, until` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `deemed accepted under s 87(1): no decision was notified within 10 working days`
    `deemed accepted under s 87(2): the deferral ran out without a decision`

GIVEN d IS A `Liability decision`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the decision is a deferral` d IF
    CONSIDER d
    WHEN `defer accepting liability` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN d IS A `Liability decision`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the decision is a dispute without the s 85(8) statement` d IF
    CONSIDER d
    WHEN `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN f IS A `Decision facts`
GIVETH A `Deemed acceptance answer`
`whether liability is deemed accepted under s 87` f MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a decision was notified in time` AND `the decision is a dispute without the s 85(8) statement` (f's `the decision`)
            THEN `not deemed: the employer notified a dispute in time, but without the s 85(8) statement, so it has not met s 85(8)`
        IF `a decision was notified in time` AND (NOT (`the decision is a deferral` (f's `the decision`)))
            THEN `not deemed: the employer notified a decision in time`
        IF `a decision was notified in time` AND `the later decision came within the deferral`
            THEN `not deemed: the employer notified a decision in time`
        IF `a decision was notified in time` AND ((f's `today`) `is on or before` `the end of the deferral`)
            THEN `not yet: the deferral is in force, until` `the end of the deferral`
        IF `a decision was notified in time`
            THEN `deemed accepted under s 87(2): the deferral ran out without a decision`
        IF ((f's `today`) `is on or before` `the last day for the decision`)
            THEN `not yet: the employer may still notify a decision, until` `the last day for the decision`
        OTHERWISE `deemed accepted under s 87(1): no decision was notified within 10 working days`
    WHERE
        `the last day for the decision` MEANS
            `the last day to notify a decision under s 85(1)` (f's `the day the employer received the claim`) (f's `weekday public holidays in the 10 working days`)
        `a decision was notified in time` MEANS
                f's `the employer has notified a decision`
            AND ((f's `the day the decision was notified or posted`) `is on or before` `the last day for the decision`)
        `the end of the deferral` MEANS `the last day of a deferral under s 85(4)(a)` (f's `the day the decision was notified or posted`)
        `the later decision came within the deferral` MEANS
                f's `after deferring, the employer has notified acceptance or a dispute`
            AND ((f's `the day that later decision was notified or posted`) `is on or before` `the end of the deferral`)

-- s 87(1)(a), (2)(a), (3): once liability is deemed accepted, a later notice ends the deemed acceptance at "the expiry of
-- 14 days after the day on which the employer notifies" a decision (or when the Court orders otherwise, s 87(1)(b),
-- (2)(b)). Only a notice that meets s 87(3) counts: given as s 85(6) requires and, for a dispute, with the s 85(8)
-- statement (s 87(3)(b)). Under (2)(a) the decision must be one "to accept or dispute liability"; under (1)(a) it is "his
-- or her decision in pursuance of that section", which might take in a deferral. Whether it does is not covered.
-- READING: the deemed acceptance runs to the 14th day after the day of the notice (or of posting, s 85(6)(c)).
DECLARE `Deemed acceptance end answer` IS ONE OF
    `the deemed acceptance runs until the expiry of 14 days after the notice, to` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `not ended by this notice: a dispute without the s 85(8) statement is not a notification for s 87(1)(a) and (2)(a) (s 87(3)(b))`
    `not covered: whether a notice of deferral ends a deemed acceptance (s 87(1)(a), (2)(a))`

GIVEN `the later decision` IS A `Liability decision`, `the day the later decision was notified or posted` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Deemed acceptance end answer`
`when a deemed acceptance ends under s 87(1)(a), (2)(a) and (3)` `the later decision` `the day the later decision was notified or posted` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the later decision`
    WHEN `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement` THEN `not ended by this notice: a dispute without the s 85(8) statement is not a notification for s 87(1)(a) and (2)(a) (s 87(3)(b))`
    WHEN `defer accepting liability` THEN `not covered: whether a notice of deferral ends a deemed acceptance (s 87(1)(a), (2)(a))`
    OTHERWISE `the deemed acceptance runs until the expiry of 14 days after the notice, to` (`the day the later decision was notified or posted` `plus days` `the s 87 days`)

-- s 87(1) with s 85(2), READING (NOTES.md finding 12): the Act sets no time to start weekly payments on a deemed acceptance.
-- s 85(2)'s 3 working days are read in, counted from the day acceptance is deemed. That is the day after the last of the
-- 10 working days, when the failure to notify in time is complete.
GIVEN `the day the employer received the claim` IS A DATE
      `weekday public holidays in the 10 working days` IS A NUMBER
      `weekday public holidays in the next 3 working days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to commence weekly payments on a deemed acceptance under s 87(1), on the s 85(2) reading` `the day the employer received the claim` `weekday public holidays in the 10 working days` `weekday public holidays in the next 3 working days` MEANS
    `the last of` `the s 85(2) working days` `working days after` `the day acceptance is deemed` `with public holidays` `weekday public holidays in the next 3 working days`
    WHERE
        `the day acceptance is deemed` MEANS
            (`the last day to notify a decision under s 85(1)` `the day the employer received the claim` `weekday public holidays in the 10 working days`) `plus days` 1

-- s 85(8), (9) and reg 13: the statement a notice of dispute must come with. Its duty is the regulative rule
-- `s 85(8) -- ...` below; s 87(3)(b) makes it a condition of a notice that ends a deemed acceptance; reg 13 adds the copy
-- and the bulletin.
DECLARE `Dispute statement facts` HAS
    `is in the approved form`                                                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `sets out the reasons in enough detail for the claimant to understand fully why`      IS A BOOLEAN
    `says the claimant may apply to the Authority for mediation within 90 days`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `says the claimant may go to the Court if mediation does not resolve the dispute`     IS A BOOLEAN
    `says a Court application must be lodged within 28 days after the mediator's certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
    `says proceedings may be started only after an unsuccessful mediation`                IS A BOOLEAN
    `says an interim determination may be sought once mediation is applied for`           IS A BOOLEAN
    `was given at the same time as the notice of the dispute`                             IS A BOOLEAN
    `came with a copy of the statement and the approved information bulletin on mediation` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN s IS A `Dispute statement facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the statement meets s 85(8) and (9)` s IF
        s's `is in the approved form`
    AND s's `sets out the reasons in enough detail for the claimant to understand fully why`
    AND s's `says the claimant may apply to the Authority for mediation within 90 days`
    AND s's `says the claimant may go to the Court if mediation does not resolve the dispute`
    AND s's `says a Court application must be lodged within 28 days after the mediator's certificate`
    AND s's `says proceedings may be started only after an unsuccessful mediation`
    AND s's `says an interim determination may be sought once mediation is applied for`
    AND s's `was given at the same time as the notice of the dispute`

GIVEN s IS A `Dispute statement facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the employer also met reg 13` s IF
    s's `came with a copy of the statement and the approved information bulletin on mediation`

-- s 89(1): if a weekly payment is late, the worker "must, in respect of that weekly payment, be paid ... an amount
-- represented by the formula: A x the prescribed rate of interest x [the rest did not survive conversion] where: A is the
-- amount of that weekly payment ...; and B is the number of weeks (with a part of a week being counted as a whole week)
-- occurring within the period commencing immediately after the day on which payment of that weekly payment was due and
-- concluding at the end of the day on which payment of that weekly payment is made." Reg 14: "the prescribed rate of
-- interest is the rate applicable to a judgment debt under a judgment of the Supreme Court" (not deposited). The amount is
-- an INPUT (NOTES.md finding 2); B is worked out here.
GIVEN `the day the payment was due` IS A DATE, `the day it was made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`B, the weeks late under s 89(1)` `the day the payment was due` `the day it was made` MEANS
    IF `the days late` AT MOST 0
    THEN 0
    ELSE FLOOR ((`the days late` PLUS 6) DIVIDED BY 7)
    WHERE
        `the days late` MEANS `days from` `the day the payment was due` `to` `the day it was made`

§§ `ss 65C, 84(5), 85A, 86, 88 -- the most the employer can be fined`

-- Penalties are in penalty units; the value of a penalty unit is set by the Penalty Units Act 2009 (NT), not deposited.
-- s 84(5): "Maximum penalty ...: In the case of a body corporate -- 85 penalty units. In the case of a natural person -- 25
-- penalty units." s 85A(2): 85 and 17. s 86(2): 25, and "8 penalty units or imprisonment for 3 months". s 88(1): 125 and
-- 25. s 65C(1): "Maximum penalty: 200 penalty units".
DECLARE `Employer offence` IS ONE OF
    `refusing to receive a claim, or dismissing a worker only for claiming (s 84(5))`
    `not giving earnings information within 21 days without reasonable excuse (s 85A(2))`
    `not deciding an application to alter weekly payments within 14 days (s 86(2))`
    `not making a weekly payment in time (s 88(1))`
    `dismissing an incapacitated worker within 6 months of the injury (s 65C(1))`

GIVEN o IS AN `Employer offence`, `the employer is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum penalty in penalty units for` o `the employer is a body corporate` MEANS
    CONSIDER o
    WHEN `refusing to receive a claim, or dismissing a worker only for claiming (s 84(5))` THEN
        IF `the employer is a body corporate` THEN 85 ELSE 25
    WHEN `not giving earnings information within 21 days without reasonable excuse (s 85A(2))` THEN
        IF `the employer is a body corporate` THEN 85 ELSE 17
    WHEN `not deciding an application to alter weekly payments within 14 days (s 86(2))` THEN
        IF `the employer is a body corporate` THEN 25 ELSE 8
    WHEN `not making a weekly payment in time (s 88(1))` THEN
        IF `the employer is a body corporate` THEN 125 ELSE 25
    WHEN `dismissing an incapacitated worker within 6 months of the injury (s 65C(1))` THEN 200

-- s 65C(4): "relevant period ... (a) that starts on the day on which the injury occurs; and (b) that ends 6 months after
-- that day." (1): an offence if, during it, the employer intentionally engages in conduct that results in the dismissal of
-- a worker incapacitated by the injury, reckless as to the incapacity; (3): a defence "if the reason for the dismissal is
-- serious misconduct by the worker"; (4): dismissal includes imposing "an unreasonable employment condition ... designed to
-- make the worker leave" where the worker leaves.
`the s 65C relevant period, in months` MEANS 6

GIVEN `the day of the injury` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day of the s 65C relevant period` `the day of the injury` MEANS
    `the day of the injury` `plus months` `the s 65C relevant period, in months`

DECLARE `Dismissal answer` IS ONE OF
    `the dismissal would be an offence under s 65C (up to 200 penalty units)`
    `not an offence under s 65C: the reason is the worker's serious misconduct (s 65C(3))`
    `not an offence under s 65C: outside the 6 months after the injury`
    `not an offence under s 65C: the worker is not incapacitated for work by the injury`

GIVEN `the worker is incapacitated for work as a result of the injury` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the reason for the dismissal is the worker's serious misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the injury` IS A DATE
      `the day of the dismissal` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Dismissal answer`
`whether a dismissal is an offence under s 65C` `the worker is incapacitated for work as a result of the injury` `the reason for the dismissal is the worker's serious misconduct` `the day of the injury` `the day of the dismissal` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the worker is incapacitated for work as a result of the injury`
            THEN `not an offence under s 65C: the worker is not incapacitated for work by the injury`
        IF NOT (`the day of the dismissal` `is on or before` (`the last day of the s 65C relevant period` `the day of the injury`))
            THEN `not an offence under s 65C: outside the 6 months after the injury`
        IF `the reason for the dismissal is the worker's serious misconduct`
            THEN `not an offence under s 65C: the reason is the worker's serious misconduct (s 65C(3))`
        OTHERWISE `the dismissal would be an offence under s 65C (up to 200 penalty units)`

§§ `ss 103D, 103J, 104 -- the worker's deadlines in a dispute`

-- s 103D(1A): if the dispute is about a decision to dispute liability or to cancel or reduce compensation, "the claimant
-- must apply under subsection (1) within 90 days of receiving the statement referred to in section 85(8) or 69(1)(b)".
-- (4), (5): a late claimant may ask the Court to extend the period, which it may do for "mistake, ignorance of a disease,
-- absence from the Territory or other reasonable cause". s 103J(1): no Court proceedings without an unsuccessful mediation;
-- (2): the mediator issues a certificate at the end. s 104(3): proceedings "are to be commenced within 28 days after the
-- claimant receives a certificate issued under section 103J(2)"; (4): the failure "shall not be a bar" for mistake,
-- ignorance of a disease, absence from the Territory or other reasonable cause. s 85(8)(d) and s 69(1)(b)(iv) only require
-- the employer's statement to say so. s 69(1)(a): 14 days' notice before cancelling or reducing s 65 compensation.
`the s 103D(1A) days` MEANS 90
`the s 104(3) days`   MEANS 28
`the s 69(1)(a) days` MEANS 14

-- s 75B(2), (3) with s 69(1): weekly compensation may be reduced or cancelled for an unreasonable failure to undertake
-- treatment, training or a return to work program, or an unreasonable refusal to attend an assessment, but "shall not be
-- cancelled or reduced unless the worker ... has been given: (a) 14 days notice of the intention ... and (b) a statement
-- in the approved form" (reasons, and the 90 days for mediation). Whether the failure was unreasonable is a finding: an
-- INPUT. READING: the 14 days' notice is complete when 14 days have passed since it was given. The employer's prohibition
-- itself is the regulative rule `s 69(1) -- ...` below; this function answers the worker's question from the same terms.
GIVEN `the failure or refusal was unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days since notice of the intended reduction or cancellation was given` IS A NUMBER
      `the s 69(1)(b) statement was given` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `weekly compensation may now be reduced or cancelled under ss 75B and 69` `the failure or refusal was unreasonable` `the days since notice of the intended reduction or cancellation was given` `the s 69(1)(b) statement was given` IF
        `the failure or refusal was unreasonable`
    AND `the days since notice of the intended reduction or cancellation was given` AT LEAST `the s 69(1)(a) days`
    AND `the s 69(1)(b) statement was given`

GIVEN `the day the statement was received` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to apply for mediation under s 103D(1A)` `the day the statement was received` MEANS
    `the day the statement was received` `plus days` `the s 103D(1A) days`

GIVEN `the day the certificate was received` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the last day to commence proceedings under s 104(3)` `the day the certificate was received` MEANS
    `the day the certificate was received` `plus days` `the s 104(3) days`

§§ `Regulative rules -- the worker and the employer`

-- Clock: days, counted from the day the rule starts (named in each rule). Working-day periods are given to a rule as the
-- calendar days they span (worked out with `the last of` ... `working days after` in rtw-dates.l4).
DECLARE `RTW party` IS ONE OF `the worker`, `the employer`

DECLARE `RTW act` IS ONE OF
    `give notice of the injury`
    `give the remaining claim document`
    `forward the claim`
    `refuse to receive the claim`
    `dismiss the worker`
    `notify the decision on liability` HAS `the decision` IS A `Liability decision`
    `give the claimant a dispute statement` HAS `the statement` IS A `Dispute statement facts`
    `commence weekly payments`
    `pay the compensation claimed`
    `provide information about the worker's weekly earnings`
    `accept the application and pay at the new level`
    `reject the application, with reasons`
    `make the weekly payment`
    `give the worker written notice of the statutory event`
    `undertake the reasonable treatment, training or assessment`
    `apply to the Authority for mediation`
    `apply to the Court to extend the time for mediation`
    `commence proceedings in the Court`
    `commence proceedings in the Court, relying on s 104(4)`
    `apply to the Authority for a reassessment`
    `pay the permanent impairment compensation`
    `make the claim`
    `make the claim, relying on s 182(3)`
    `cancel or reduce the weekly compensation`

-- s 80(1): "a person shall not be entitled to compensation unless notice of the relevant injury has, as soon as
-- practicable, been given to or served on the worker's employer". What was practicable is a judgment: an INPUT, in days
-- from the injury. (2): a claim counts as notice.
GIVEN `the days within which notice was practicable` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 80(1) -- the worker must give notice of the injury as soon as practicable` `the days within which notice was practicable` IS
    PARTY `the worker`
    MUST  `give notice of the injury`
    WITHIN `the days within which notice was practicable`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the worker` BECAUSE "s 80(1): no compensation unless notice of the injury was given to the employer as soon as practicable; a claim counts as notice (s 80(2)), and s 182(2) can excuse the want of notice"

-- s 82(2), from the day the first of the claim and the certificate was given.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 82(2) -- the worker must give the remaining claim document within 28 days` IS
    PARTY `the worker`
    MUST  `give the remaining claim document`
    WITHIN `the s 82(2) period, in days`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the worker` BECAUSE "s 82(2): the claim and the medical certificate of capacity were not both given within 28 days; the claim is taken not to have been made until the remaining document is given"

-- s 182(1), (3), from the day the 6 months run from (the injury; for a disease, the incapacity from it; on a death, the
-- day the claimant received advice of the death), given to the rule as the calendar days to the last day to claim
-- (`the last day to claim under s 182(1)`). Without a claim in time, proceedings "shall not be maintainable", unless the
-- failure "was occasioned by mistake, ignorance of a disease, absence from the Territory or other reasonable cause"
-- (s 182(3), (5)): missing the deadline leaves only that saving, not a breach. On a death claim "the worker" is the
-- claimant. The notice condition of s 182(1) and (2) is in `whether proceedings on the claim are maintainable under s 182`.
GIVEN `the days to the last day to claim` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 182(1) -- the worker must make the claim within 6 months` `the days to the last day to claim` IS
    PARTY `the worker`
    DO    `make the claim`
    WITHIN `the days to the last day to claim`
    LEST  PARTY `the worker`
          MAY   `make the claim, relying on s 182(3)`

-- The calendar days that the working-day periods of ss 84, 85 and 87 span in a given case.
DECLARE `Working-day spans` HAS
    `calendar days to the 3rd working day after the claim was received`  IS A NUMBER
    `calendar days to the 10th working day after the claim was received` IS A NUMBER
    `calendar days to the 3rd working day after the decision`            IS A NUMBER
    `calendar days to the 3rd working day after the day acceptance is deemed` IS A NUMBER

-- s 87(1): deemed acceptance of liability for Subdivisions B and D compensation, from the day acceptance is deemed: the day
-- after the last of the 10 working days in s 85(1). The Act sets no time to start weekly payments on a deemed acceptance.
-- READING: s 85(2)'s 3 working days, counted from that day (NOTES.md finding 12; the date is
-- `the last day to commence weekly payments on a deemed acceptance under s 87(1), on the s 85(2) reading`). For a claim not
-- for weekly payments, the compensation claimed is owed (s 85(3): "as soon as practicable").
-- In a trace this rule is under the LEST of the 10 working days, and L4 starts it when the trace first notices that they
-- have passed. A trace must therefore reach the day acceptance is deemed with a WAITUNTIL, or the clock starts late.
GIVEN `the claim is for weekly payments` IS A BOOLEAN, spans IS A `Working-day spans`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
`s 87 -- liability is deemed accepted` `the claim is for weekly payments` spans MEANS
    IF `the claim is for weekly payments`
    THEN PARTY `the employer`
         MUST  `commence weekly payments`
         WITHIN spans's `calendar days to the 3rd working day after the day acceptance is deemed`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 87(1): no decision was notified within 10 working days, so liability is deemed accepted for weekly payments and medical and other costs, and weekly payments must start (READING: within 3 working days of the day acceptance is deemed, as s 85(2)); the deemed acceptance runs until 14 days after the employer notifies a decision as s 87(3) requires, or the Court orders otherwise"
    ELSE PARTY `the employer`
         MUST  `pay the compensation claimed`

-- s 85(8), (9), from the moment the employer notifies a dispute: "At the same time as an employer notifies a claimant
-- under this section that the employer disputes liability for compensation claimed, the employer must give the claimant a
-- statement in the approved form" with the reasons (in enough detail to understand fully why, s 85(9)) and the claimant's
-- mediation and Court rights. READING: on this encoding's day clock, "at the same time" is the same day (WITHIN 0). The
-- Act attaches no penalty and no deemed acceptance to a failure (NOTES.md finding 18). Without the statement the
-- claimant's 90 days for mediation have not started (s 103D(1A)), and a later dispute ends a deemed acceptance only with
-- it (s 87(3)(b)).
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 85(8) -- with a notice of dispute, the employer must give the claimant the statement in the approved form` IS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST  `give the claimant a dispute statement` `the statement` PROVIDED `the statement meets s 85(8) and (9)` `the statement`
    WITHIN 0
    LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 85(8), (9): the employer disputed liability without giving, at the same time, the statement in the approved form with full reasons and the claimant's mediation and Court rights; the dispute is still a decision notified under s 85(1), but the 90 days for mediation run only from receiving the statement (s 103D(1A))"

-- What a notified decision still requires under s 85(8): nothing, unless it is a dispute without the statement.
GIVEN d IS A `Liability decision`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
`what s 85(8) requires after the decision` d MEANS
    CONSIDER d
    WHEN `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement` THEN `s 85(8) -- with a notice of dispute, the employer must give the claimant the statement in the approved form`
    OTHERWISE FULFILLED

-- s 85(4)(a), (b) and s 87(2), from the day the deferral is notified: weekly payments within 3 working days, and a final
-- decision within the 56 days. s 85(4)(a) ends the deferral when the employer "notifies the person making the claim that
-- the employer accepts or disputes liability", and s 87(2) turns on a failure "to make a decision to accept or dispute
-- liability", so a dispute without the s 85(8) statement ends it too, with the s 85(8) duty unmet (s 87(3) does not reach
-- this; NOTES.md finding 18). The trace's BREACH on the second marks the s 87(2) failure, whose consequence is deemed
-- acceptance; payments already started continue (s 85(7)(b)).
GIVEN d IS A `Liability decision`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the decision ends a deferral` d IF
    CONSIDER d
    WHEN `accept liability` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `dispute liability, with the s 85(8) statement` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the claim is for weekly payments` IS A BOOLEAN, spans IS A `Working-day spans`, `the decision` IS A `Liability decision`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
`s 85 -- what the employer must do after its decision` `the claim is for weekly payments` spans `the decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `accept liability` THEN
        IF `the claim is for weekly payments`
        THEN PARTY `the employer`
             MUST  `commence weekly payments`
             WITHIN spans's `calendar days to the 3rd working day after the decision`
             LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 85(2): on accepting a claim for weekly payments, the employer must start them within 3 working days"
        ELSE PARTY `the employer`
             MUST  `pay the compensation claimed`
    WHEN `defer accepting liability` THEN
        IF `the claim is for weekly payments`
        THEN    (PARTY `the employer`
                 MUST  `commence weekly payments`
                 WITHIN spans's `calendar days to the 3rd working day after the decision`
                 LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 85(4)(b): on deferring a claim for weekly payments, the employer must start them within 3 working days")
            RAND
                (PARTY `the employer`
                 MUST  `notify the decision on liability` `the final decision` PROVIDED `the decision ends a deferral` `the final decision`
                 WITHIN `the s 85(4)(a) days`
                 HENCE `what s 85(8) requires after the decision` `the final decision`
                 LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 85(4)(a), 87(2): the deferral ran out after 56 days without a decision to accept or dispute; liability is deemed accepted for weekly payments and medical and other costs, and the payments continue (s 85(7)(b))")
        ELSE PARTY `the employer`
             MUST  `notify the decision on liability` `the final decision` PROVIDED `the decision ends a deferral` `the final decision`
             WITHIN `the s 85(4)(a) days`
             HENCE `what s 85(8) requires after the decision` `the final decision`
             LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 85(4)(a), 87(2): the deferral ran out after 56 days without a decision to accept or dispute; liability is deemed accepted for medical and other costs"
    WHEN `dispute liability, with the s 85(8) statement` THEN FULFILLED
    WHEN `dispute liability, without the s 85(8) statement` THEN `s 85(8) -- with a notice of dispute, the employer must give the claimant the statement in the approved form`

-- ss 84(1), 85(1), 87, from the day the employer receives the claim.
GIVEN `the claim is for weekly payments` IS A BOOLEAN, spans IS A `Working-day spans`
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `ss 84(1), 85, 87 -- the employer must forward the claim, decide it in time and pay on acceptance or deferral` `the claim is for weekly payments` spans IS
        (PARTY `the employer`
         MUST  `forward the claim`
         WITHIN spans's `calendar days to the 3rd working day after the claim was received`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 84(1): the employer must forward the claim to its insurer (or, if uninsured, the Nominal Insurer; if self-insured, a copy to the Authority) within 3 working days; the worker may lodge the claim with the insurer or the Authority instead (s 84(3))")
    RAND
        (PARTY `the employer`
         MUST  `notify the decision on liability` `the decision`
         WITHIN spans's `calendar days to the 10th working day after the claim was received`
         HENCE `s 85 -- what the employer must do after its decision` `the claim is for weekly payments` spans `the decision`
         LEST  `s 87 -- liability is deemed accepted` `the claim is for weekly payments` spans)

-- s 84(5): an employer who "(a) refuses to receive a claim for compensation; or (b) dismisses a worker from employment for
-- the reason only that the worker has given or attempted to give to the employer or the Authority a claim for
-- compensation, is guilty of an offence". No period is stated: the prohibition is traced over the given number of days.
GIVEN `the days considered` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 84(5) -- the employer must not refuse to receive a claim, or dismiss the worker for claiming` `the days considered` IS
        (PARTY `the employer`
         SHANT `refuse to receive the claim`
         WITHIN `the days considered`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 84(5)(a): refusing to receive a claim is an offence (up to 85 penalty units for a body corporate, 25 for a natural person); the worker may lodge the claim with the insurer or the Authority (s 84(3)(b))")
    RAND
        (PARTY `the employer`
         SHANT `dismiss the worker`
         WITHIN `the days considered`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 84(5)(b): dismissing a worker only for giving or trying to give a claim is an offence (up to 85 penalty units for a body corporate, 25 for a natural person)")

-- s 85A(2): "An employer must, in the absence of reasonable excuse, comply with a request under subsection (1) within 21
-- days after receiving the request." From the day the request is received.
GIVEN `the employer has a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 85A(2) -- the employer must give the worker information about weekly earnings within 21 days` `the employer has a reasonable excuse` IS
    IF `the employer has a reasonable excuse`
    THEN FULFILLED
    ELSE PARTY `the employer`
         MUST  `provide information about the worker's weekly earnings`
         WITHIN `the s 85A(2) days`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 85A(2): the employer must give the requested earnings information within 21 days (up to 85 penalty units for a body corporate, 17 for a natural person)"

-- s 86(2), (3), from the day the application is received: accept it and pay at the new level, or reject it with reasons,
-- within 14 days.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 86 -- the employer must decide an application to alter weekly payments within 14 days` IS
        (PARTY `the employer`
         MUST  `accept the application and pay at the new level`
         WITHIN `the s 86(2) days`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 86(2), (3): the employer must accept or reject the application within 14 days, and pay at the new level within that period if it accepts (up to 25 penalty units for a body corporate, 8 or 3 months' imprisonment for a natural person)")
    ROR
        (PARTY `the employer`
         MUST  `reject the application, with reasons`
         WITHIN `the s 86(2) days`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 86(2): the employer must accept or reject the application within 14 days, giving reasons for a rejection (up to 25 penalty units for a body corporate, 8 or 3 months' imprisonment for a natural person)")

-- s 88(1), from the end of the week (or the normal pay period) the payment is for. The worker may agree in writing to a
-- different time: give its days instead of 7.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 88(1) -- the employer must make each weekly payment within 7 days after the end of the week` `the days allowed` IS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST  `make the weekly payment`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 88(1): the weekly payment is late; the worker must also be paid interest under s 89 at the Supreme Court judgment-debt rate (reg 14), and the employer commits an offence (up to 125 penalty units for a body corporate, 25 for a natural person)"

-- s 61B(2), from the day the employer learns the statutory event is about to occur: written notice of its date and
-- effect. No period is stated; a notice given less than 14 days before the event only delays the change (s 61B(4)).
GIVEN `the days until the statutory event` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 61B -- the employer must give written notice of a statutory event before it occurs` `the days until the statutory event` IS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST  `give the worker written notice of the statutory event`
    WITHIN `the days until the statutory event`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 61B(2), (4): no notice was given before the statutory event; the change to compensation takes effect only 14 days after the notice is given"

-- s 65C, from the day of the injury.
GIVEN `the worker is incapacitated for work as a result of the injury` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the reason would be the worker's serious misconduct` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days in the relevant period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 65C -- the employer must not dismiss an incapacitated worker within 6 months of the injury` `the worker is incapacitated for work as a result of the injury` `the reason would be the worker's serious misconduct` `the days in the relevant period` IS
    IF (NOT `the worker is incapacitated for work as a result of the injury`) OR `the reason would be the worker's serious misconduct`
    THEN FULFILLED
    ELSE PARTY `the employer`
         SHANT `dismiss the worker`
         WITHIN `the days in the relevant period`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 65C(1): dismissing a worker incapacitated by the injury within 6 months of it is an offence (up to 200 penalty units), unless the reason is serious misconduct (s 65C(3))"

-- s 75B(1): where s 64 or 65 compensation is payable, "the worker shall undertake, at the expense of the worker's employer,
-- reasonable medical, surgical and rehabilitation treatment or participate in rehabilitation training or ... workplace
-- based return to work programs, or as required by his or her employer, present himself or herself at reasonable
-- intervals ... for assessment". (2), (3): an unreasonable failure or refusal deems the worker able to work, and
-- compensation "may, subject to section 69, be reduced or cancelled". The time that was reasonable is an INPUT, in days.
GIVEN `the days within which it was reasonable to undertake it` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 75B -- the worker must undertake reasonable treatment, rehabilitation or assessment` `the days within which it was reasonable to undertake it` IS
    PARTY `the worker`
    MUST  `undertake the reasonable treatment, training or assessment`
    WITHIN `the days within which it was reasonable to undertake it`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the worker` BECAUSE "s 75B(2), (3): an unreasonable failure deems the worker able to do the work it would have enabled, and weekly compensation may be reduced or cancelled after 14 days' notice and a statement under s 69"

-- s 69(1), from the day the worker is given notice of the intention to cancel or reduce compensation under Subdivision B
-- (ss 64, 65): it "shall not be cancelled or reduced unless the worker to whom it is payable has been given: (a) 14 days
-- notice of the intention to cancel or reduce the compensation and, where the compensation is to be reduced, the amount to
-- which it is to be reduced; and (b) a statement in the approved form" (the reasons, in enough detail to understand fully
-- why, s 69(4); the 90 days for mediation; the appeal to the Court). The prohibition falls on whoever pays: the employer.
-- READING, as for `weekly compensation may now be reduced or cancelled under ss 75B and 69`: the 14 days' notice is
-- complete on the 14th day after it was given, so cancelling or reducing is barred to the 13th day. Where the statement
-- has not been given, the bar has no end in the text: it is traced over the given number of days. Section 69(2) (return
-- to work, death, no certificate when asked, fraud, a Court order) is not encoded.
GIVEN `the s 69(1)(b) statement has been given` IS A BOOLEAN, `the days considered` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 69(1) -- the employer must not cancel or reduce weekly compensation without 14 days' notice and the statement` `the s 69(1)(b) statement has been given` `the days considered` IS
    IF `the s 69(1)(b) statement has been given`
    THEN PARTY `the employer`
         SHANT `cancel or reduce the weekly compensation`
         WITHIN (`the s 69(1)(a) days` MINUS 1)
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 69(1)(a): weekly compensation was cancelled or reduced before the worker had 14 days' notice of the intention (and of the reduced amount)"
    ELSE PARTY `the employer`
         SHANT `cancel or reduce the weekly compensation`
         WITHIN `the days considered`
         LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 69(1)(b): weekly compensation was cancelled or reduced without the statement in the approved form giving the reasons and the worker's right to mediation within 90 days"

-- s 103D(1A), (4), (5), from the day the s 85(8) or s 69(1)(b) statement is received. Applying is the claimant's choice;
-- missing the 90 days leaves only an application to the Court to extend them.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 103D(1A) -- the worker must apply for mediation within 90 days of the statement` IS
    PARTY `the worker`
    DO    `apply to the Authority for mediation`
    WITHIN `the s 103D(1A) days`
    LEST  PARTY `the worker`
          MAY   `apply to the Court to extend the time for mediation`

-- s 104(3), (4), from the day the mediator's s 103J(2) certificate is received.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 104(3) -- the worker must commence Court proceedings within 28 days of the mediator's certificate` IS
    PARTY `the worker`
    DO    `commence proceedings in the Court`
    WITHIN `the s 104(3) days`
    LEST  PARTY `the worker`
          MAY   `commence proceedings in the Court, relying on s 104(4)`

-- s 72(3), from the day the worker is notified of the assessment.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 72(3) -- the worker may apply for a reassessment within 28 days` IS
    PARTY `the worker`
    MAY   `apply to the Authority for a reassessment`
    WITHIN `the s 72(3) days`

-- s 71(4)(a), from the day the worker is notified of the assessment, where no reassessment is sought: 28 + 14 days.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 71(4)(a) -- with no reassessment sought, the employer must pay within 14 days after the 28 days` IS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST  `pay the permanent impairment compensation`
    WITHIN (`the s 72(3) days` PLUS `the s 71(4)(a) days`)
    LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 71(4)(a): the permanent impairment compensation must be paid within 14 days after the 28 days allowed to seek a reassessment; the registrar must certify an unpaid amount for judgment in the Local Court (s 72A)"

-- s 71(4)(b), from the day the applicant is notified of the reassessment.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `RTW party` `RTW act`
DECIDE `s 71(4)(b) -- after a reassessment, the employer must pay within 28 days of the notice` IS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST  `pay the permanent impairment compensation`
    WITHIN `the s 71(4)(b) days`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "s 71(4)(b): the permanent impairment compensation must be paid within 28 days after the reassessment is notified; the registrar must certify an unpaid amount for judgment in the Local Court (s 72A)"
